 (minimum 200), All-in\n- This is hand 9 of 20, and I'm ahead by 2,650 cumulative chips\n\n**Hand Strength Analysis:**\nA-J offsuit is a decent starting hand - it's in the top 15-20% of all starting hands. The Ace gives me the highest card, and having a Jack kicker is reasonably strong. This hand has good potential to make top pair with a strong kicker, and can hit straights (10-J-Q-K-A).\n\n**Evaluation:**\n\n1. **Fold (-1)**: Too conservative. A-J is strong enough to play, especially heads-up. Folding would waste a playable hand.\n\n2. **Check (0)**: This appears to be the big blind position since checking is allowed pre-flop. Checking is passive but allows me to see the flop cheaply and avoid committing chips without seeing community cards. Given uncertainty about opponent's hand, this is safe.\n\n3. **Raise (200+)**: A standard raise would build the pot with a strong hand and put pressure on opponent. With A-J, a raise of 200-400 chips (2-4x BB assuming 100 BB) would be standard. This shows strength and can win the pot immediately if opponent folds weak hands.\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Very aggressive and high-risk with A-J offsuit pre-flop. While strong, it's not strong enough to justify risking all chips before seeing any community cards in a 2-player game.\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n- I'm currently winning overall (+2,650 cumulative)\n- A-J offsuit is strong enough to play aggressively but not premium enough for all-in\n- A moderate raise balances aggression with risk management\n- Early in the session (hand 9 of 20), so no need for desperate plays\n\n**Reason:** A-J offsuit is a strong starting hand that warrants aggression, but not reckless over-commitment. A standard raise accomplishes multiple goals: builds the pot with a likely stronger hand, applies pressure on the opponent, and maintains control while limiting risk. Given my chip lead, making solid +EV plays is better than gambling everything.\n\n**Answer:** 300"}
