ard: 3 of Hearts, 9 of Hearts, 10 of Diamonds\n- This is the Flop (3 community cards revealed)\n- I'm in the lead overall (+2650 cumulative)\n- Current chips: 19,650\n\n**Hand Analysis:**\n- I have Ace-high (high card)\n- I have a gutshot straight draw (need a Queen or King to make a straight: A-J-10-9 or J-10-9-K)\n- I have a backdoor flush draw in hearts (J of Hearts on board with 3 and 9 of Hearts)\n- Currently, I only have Ace-high - a relatively weak hand\n\n**Pre-flop Action:**\n- I raised 300 pre-flop (showing strength)\n- Opponent called (showing interest but not aggressive)\n\n**Promising Moves:**\n1. **Check (0):** Free card to see the Turn. Low risk, but gives opponent free card too. Since I raised pre-flop, checking might signal weakness.\n\n2. **Small continuation bet (~400-600):** Maintains pressure from my pre-flop raise. Represents strength and may take down the pot if opponent missed the flop.\n\n3. **Larger bet (~1000-1500):** More aggressive continuation bet. Higher risk but could force opponent to fold marginal hands.\n\n4. **Fold (-1):** Too weak given I only have Ace-high and no clear path to improvement. However, folding after raising pre-flop seems premature.\n\n**Opponent Considerations:**\n- They just called pre-flop, suggesting a medium-strength hand\n- The flop (3-9-10 rainbow) is relatively dry and unlikely to have hit most hands hard\n- A continuation bet is standard after raising pre-flop\n\nReason:\nA continuation bet of around 500-700 chips is optimal here. I raised pre-flop showing strength, and this flop is unlikely to have connected strongly with my opponent's calling range. A moderate bet maintains the narrative of my hand strength while not overcommitting with just Ace-high. If called, I can reassess on the Turn. If raised, I can fold without losing too much. This bet size (about 1/3 to 1/2 pot) is standard for a continuation bet and keeps pressure on the opponent.\n\nAnswer: 600"}
