ly from 200 to 400 total, given standard blind structure)\n- Opponent went all-in (-2)\n- My chips: 19,550\n- Opponent's chips: 20,000 (since we each start with 20,000 per hand)\n\n**Hand Strength Analysis:**\n- Pocket Jacks is a premium starting hand, typically ranked as the 4th or 5th best starting hand in Texas Hold'em\n- Pre-flop, JJ has roughly:\n  - ~82% equity vs any random hand\n  - ~55-57% equity vs hands like AK or AQ (which are common all-in hands)\n  - Only loses to higher pairs: QQ, KK, AA (about 18% chance opponent has these)\n\n**Pot Odds:**\n- If I call all-in with my 19,550 chips\n- Total pot would be ~39,550\n- I need to risk 19,550 to win the pot\n- I need roughly 49.4% equity to break even\n- JJ has well above 49.4% equity against most hands\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n- This is hand 17 of 20 - near the end of the match\n- I'm ahead by 1,000 cumulative chips\n- Folding means I lose 400 chips this hand, maintaining my lead\n- Calling and winning would give me a massive chip advantage\n- Calling and losing would put me significantly behind\n\n**Opponent's Range:**\nAn all-in pre-flop typically indicates: AA, KK, QQ, AK suited/unsuited, or occasionally JJ, TT, or bluffs with suited connectors.\n\nReason:\nDespite the risk, pocket Jacks is too strong to fold pre-flop to an all-in. Even against premium hands like AK (which is a common all-in hand), I'm roughly a coin flip favorite. Against lower pairs or bluffs, I dominate. Only QQ, KK, and AA have me crushed, and the probability opponent holds exactly one of these three hands is relatively low. With approximately 55-65% equity against a typical all-in range, the pot odds strongly favor calling. While I could fold to preserve my lead, being too conservative with such a strong hand would be a strategic error that could be exploited.\n\nAnswer: -2"}
